  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe boundary that pushed through our area this weekend is stalled\noffshore and will help to keep the focus of convection into our\noffshore waters over the next couple of days. We could get an\nisolated shower into the coastal bend as we saw yesterday afternoon,\nbut this will remain very limited. Ultimated, this boundary will \nalso help to steer any potential tropical system away from the \nTexas coast late this week and into the weekend. There is \ncurrently an 80% of development by late this week, with guidance \nnow in general agreement of an east and then northern track toward\nthe northern Gulf Coast. The main potential impact to our area \nwould be increased swells that could result in higher tide levels \n(coastal flooding) and an increased potential for rip currents. \nWe'll have to wait and see how things play out, but this system \ncould also bring an increase in temperatures to our area by late \nin the week as drier air wraps around from the north. Until then, \nhigh temperatures will generally be in the upper 80s and lower 90s\n\nWe're still monitoring coastal flooding early this week, and it\nhas been a challenge. Despite tide levels reaching 2ft above MSL\nalong the coast, water did not push far up the beaches yesterday.\nSuspect this is at least in part due to short (5-7 second) swell \nperiods. Longer periods have more energy and are able to push up \nthe beach farther. Wave periods are expected to continue to be \nlow, so its possible coastal flooding will be minimal, but the \ntide heights, approaching new moon and a seasonal high time \nwarrant precautions.",
